        },
        {
          "id": 681909696,
          "type": "DiscussionNote",
          "body": "Actually, I would make this two part, and allow the user to either supply the path to the homebrew installation or use the automatic approach. If we only search for brew, we a. might not find it, as it may not be in the path during the initial cmake run and subsequently runs may fail on older versions due to internally kept variables and b. if you have multiple brew installations on your system, you will only find the one in path.\n\nA case for the latter is exactly the Apple Silicon plattform, in where if you want to build for arm64 as well as x86 on the same machine, you will have two brew installations, and also have to ensure before running cmake to select the correct path variables. Which is fine by me, but it may lead to weird error scenarios, the casual user may have a hard time to solve.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2498449,
            "username": "rknall",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "Roland Knall",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/9e1d973c5007651b2ca47071f80324c2de5349d873e365cf0761df5542dfe382?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/rknall"
